# Line-ending rules.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# --------------------
# core.autocrlf=true (the Windows default) writes CRLF into the working
# tree at checkout.  For most files that is harmless: the C "clean" filter
# normalises them back to LF on commit, so the blobs stay correct.
#
# Shell scripts are the exception, and they fail *loudly* on Linux:
#
#     $ bash tools/build-linux.sh
#     tools/build-linux.sh: line 20: set: pipefail: invalid option name
#
# because "set -euo pipefail" becomes "set -euo pipefail\r" and bash reads
# the option name as "pipefail\r".  Nothing about the file looks wrong --
# the script simply refuses to run, and only when executed by a real
# Linux bash (MSYS2 tolerates it, which is why it goes unnoticed on
# Windows and only shows up in WSL or CI).
#
# Every .sh blob in this repository is already pure LF, so pinning the
# working tree to LF changes no committed byte: this rule is a checkout
# instruction, not a content rewrite.  It also means a contributor who
# clones on Windows and builds in WSL gets working scripts.
#
# Verified: after adding this, `git status` reports no modification to any
# pre-existing file -- only this file is new.

*.sh            text eol=lf

# Files that must never be line-converted at all.  Git detects most of
# these by content, but stating it removes the guesswork for any asset
# that happens to lack a NUL byte.
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.jpeg          binary
*.gif           binary
*.webp          binary
*.ico           binary
*.icns          binary
*.exe           binary
*.dll           binary
*.a             binary
*.o             binary
*.obj           binary
*.lib           binary
*.zip           binary
*.tar.gz        binary
*.tgz           binary
*.7z            binary
*.pdf           binary
*.ttf           binary
*.woff          binary
*.woff2         binary
